<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque is a mosque on the eastern side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, built under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> in the early seventeenth century. It is known for the tilework of its dome and interior. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a> cite it as part of Isfahan's architectural ensemble.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sheikh-lotfollah-mosque.jpg" alt="Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolas Hadjisavvas · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sheikh_Lotfallah_Esfahan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Construction began in 1011 AH (1602/1603 CE) and finished in 1028 AH (1618/1619 CE). The architect was Muhammad Reza, son of Husayn, a builder from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> whose name appears on an inscription around the mihrab. The mosque was meant for the royal court alone, unlike the public <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-mosque">Shah Mosque</a>, so it has no minarets and is smaller. Shah Abbas had a tunnel built from the Ali Qapu Palace under the square to it, ending in a winding passage with guards, apparently to shield the women of the harem.</p><p>It was named in 1622 after Shaykh Lutfallah Maysi al-Amili, a prominent scholar and father-in-law of Shah Abbas, who came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> at the shah's orders and lived on the site but was never involved in the building. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Shah Pahlavi</a> had the mosque restored in the 1920s, on the advice of Arthur Upham Pope.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The building is closer to a mausoleum in type than to a four-iwan mosque, which historians ascribe either to private worship by the shah's family or to its use as a women's sanctuary. It has a single domed chamber, 19 metres on a side, with no courtyard and no interior iwans. Because the square runs north to south and the qibla lies to the south-west, the architect joined the entrance to the chamber by an L-shaped vestibule, so the dome stands 6.5 metres off the axis of the portal iwan as seen from the square.</p><p>The visitor passes through two dim corridors before facing the mihrab in a room lit by nearly a score of windows. The dome, one of the few single-shell domes of Safavid architecture, is 13 metres across. Concentric rings of lozenge-shaped tilework shrink towards a central sunburst, often called a peacock because the light through the opening forms its tail.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The mosque is regarded as a masterpiece of Safavid architecture and is registered with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-jahan-square">Naqsh-e Jahan Square</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. It was added to the Iran National Heritage List on 6 January 1932. In The Road to Oxiana, Robert Byron wrote that he knew of no finer example of the Persian Islamic genius than the interior of the dome. The dome's design is said to derive from the Shah Vali Mosque in Taft, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Isfahan's ensemble of Naqsh-e Jahan Square, the Shah Mosque, the <b>Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque</b> and the Ali Qapu palace is one of the supreme achievements of Islamic urban design.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
